+ * a derivative work of XNU), which describes the situation and the protocol:
+ *
+ *   A surface is a piece of wired kernel memory: one page of state that clients read directly,
+ *   followed by the pixels. Every client that knows a surface has the whole of it mapped once.
+ *   Surfaces travel between processes as Mach send rights (IOSurfaceSendRight) or by number.
+ *   [...] What is not here: child surfaces, wrapping of client memory in pieces, purgeability,
+ *   compression, notifications. Calls that are not implemented are logged once and refused.
+ *
+ * The iOS 26 layouts (the 0xc68-byte surface description, the 0x28-byte init reply) are kept
+ * as hakios has them: iOS 27's IOSurface.framework is assumed to be the same until a boot says
+ * otherwise, and a size seen for the first time is logged.
+ *
+ * Boot-args: fuxi_surf_trace=N logs the first N surfaces made (default 24); fuxi_surf_trace_of=NAME
+ * counts only those of programs whose name begins with NAME, and says what becomes of them;
+ * fuxi_surf_clear=0 gives kept memory out uncleared (to measure what the clearing costs).
